 "endCard": 70.7667,
 "end": {
  "title": "Next drop: the spinning *chair*",
  "follow": "Follow Rest Frame",
  "foot": "The feather was a falcon's. Apollo 15's lunar module was named Falcon."
 },
 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 3.595,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Which lands first? A geology hammer, or a falcon feather?"
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 4.4,
   "dur": 7.215,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "On Earth, it's no contest. From shoulder height, the hammer lands in about half a second. The feather? Still drifting down."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 12.366666666666667,
   "dur": 11.455,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Now take the air away. On the Moon, in nineteen seventy-one, astronaut David Scott dropped both. They landed together. \"Mister Galileo was correct in his findings,\" he said."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 24.533333333333335,
   "dur": 14.114,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "For nearly two thousand years, most scholars followed Aristotle: heavier things fall faster. Gravity does pull the hammer harder. But a heavier thing is harder to speed up, by exactly as much. So without air, they tie."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 41.1,
   "dur": 9.947,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "How exact is exactly? Starting in twenty sixteen, a satellite let two metals fall around Earth for months. They matched to within a few parts in a million billion."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 54.333333333333336,
   "dur": 6.074,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "Measure it: on the Moon, that drop takes about one point four seconds. On Earth, under six tenths."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 61.36666666666667,
   "dur": 3.809,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "Gravity speeds them up equally. Only the air tells them apart."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 65.9,
   "dur": 4.726,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: a spinning office chair. Pull in your arms, and it spins faster. Why?"
  }
 ]
}